quently the remaining line is *non\u2011sunny* and is parallel to one of those three directions.  \nDenote the three lines by \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) where \\(L_{3}\\) is the non\u2011sunny line.  \nWe consider the three possibilities for \\(L_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(L_{3}\\) horizontal:  \\(y=c\\)\n\nThe only values of \\(c\\) that contain points of \\(T_{3}\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are  \n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Compute the slopes between any two points of \\(U\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{slope}\\big((1,2),(2,2)\\big)=0,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{slope}\\big((1,2),(1,3)\\big)=\\infty,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{slope}\\big((2,2),(1,3)\\big)=-1 .\n  \\]\n  All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n  Hence no sunny line can contain two points of \\(U\\); each point would need its own line, contradicting the existence of only two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Slopes among points of \\(U\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1)-(2,1):0,\\quad (1,1)-(3,1):0,\\quad (2,1)-(3,1):0,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(1,3):\\infty,\\quad (2,1)-(1,3):-1,\\quad (3,1)-(1,3)=-1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Again every pair has a forbidden slope, so a sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(U\\).  \n  At least three sunny lines would be required \u2013 impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains only \\((1,3)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  The only admissible slope among pairs of \\(U\\) is that of \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), which is \\(1\\).  \n  Thus a sunny line can contain at most the two points \\((1,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The three remaining points \\((1,2),(2,1),(3,1)\\) are pairwise connected by forbidden slopes (vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\)), so they cannot be covered by a single sunny line.  \n  Hence a third line would be needed, contradiction.\n\nTherefore no horizontal non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L_{3}\\) vertical:  \\(x=c\\)\n\nThe admissible constants are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Slopes:\n  \\[\n  (2,1)-(3,1):0,\\qquad (2,1)-(2,2):\\infty,\\qquad (3,1)-(2,2):-1 .\n  \\]\n  All are forbidden, so each point would need its own sunny line \u2013 impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(3,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Allowed slopes among pairs of \\(U\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{slope}\\big((3,1),(1,2)\\big)=-\\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(the only admissible one)}.\n  \\]  \n  Thus a sunny line can at most contain the two points \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n  The two remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are vertically aligned, giving slope \\(\\infty\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \n  Consequently a second sunny line cannot cover both, and a third line would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(1,3)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  As in the horizontal case \\(c=3\\), the only admissible pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  After covering those two points, three points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,3)\\) remain, all pairwise connected by forbidden slopes.  \n  Hence a third line would be necessary \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus no vertical non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L_{3}\\) diagonal with slope \\(-1\\):  \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nPossible constants are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  The only admissible slope among pairs of \\(U\\) is that of \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(0\\) \u2013 forbidden) or \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 forbidden).  \n  In fact **no** pair of points of \\(U\\) has an admissible slope; every pair yields slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(U\\); at least three sunny lines would be needed \u2013 impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(3,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Slopes:\n  \\[\n  (1,1)-(2,2):1\\quad\\text{(admissible)},\n  \\]\n  while all other pairs have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n  Thus one sunny line can cover at most \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n  The two remaining points \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \n  Consequently a second sunny line cannot cover both, and a third line would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:\n  \\[\n  U=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  Their pairwise slopes are\n  \\[\n  (1,1)-(1,2):\\infty,\\qquad (1,1)-(2,1):0,\\qquad (1,2)-(2,1):-1 .\n  \\]\n  All are forbidden, so no sunny line can contain two of these three points.  \n  Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus no diagonal non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible placement of the unique non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, there is **no** set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]"
