            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Number of required points  \n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?  \n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) and meets \\(T_n\\) in the\npoints \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\) points occur.\nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) is analogous.\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); for\n\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) it contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.\nHence any non‑sunny line contains **at most \\(n\\) points** of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(s\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf the line meets two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) then\n\\[\ns=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(s\\neq-1\\), the two points cannot satisfy \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\); thus\nall points of a sunny line have distinct values of \\(a+b\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can intersect at most one point on each\nanti‑diagonal \\(a+b=c\\).  \nThere are \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals intersecting \\(T_n\\) (those with\n\\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)), and the longest diagonal that a sunny line can meet\nis the one of slope \\(1\\) (equation \\(y=x+d\\)).  \nSuch a line meets the anti‑diagonals \\(c=2d+2,2d+3,\\dots ,2d+n+1\\); the number\nof intersected anti‑diagonals is at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\big\\rfloor\\).\nHence **any sunny line contains at most**\n\\[\nm:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition exists and that exactly \\(k\\ge2\\) of them are sunny.\nUsing the bounds from §2, the total number of points that can be covered is\nat most  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\max}=(n-k)\\,n+k\\,m .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, so \\(C_{\\max}\\ge|T_n|\\).\nInsert (5) and (6) into this inequality:\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,m\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nRewrite (8) as  \n\n\\[\nn^2-nk+km\\ge\\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-m)} .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\big\\rfloor\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nn-m=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}2\\Big\\rceil\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nA short case analysis gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-m)}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{2t(2t-1)}{2t}=2t-1<2t & (n=2t\\text{ even}),\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{(2t+1)2t}{2(t+1)}= \\frac{2t(2t+1)}{2(t+1)}<2 & (n=2t+1\\text{ odd}),\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nso the right–hand side of (9) is **strictly smaller than 2** for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently (9) forces  \n\n\\[\nk<2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist** for any\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on\n\\(L_a\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.\nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the single sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y-2=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-1),\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the line through the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\nIts slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is\nsunny.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-1\\), then it lies on the\nvertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* The only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); by construction\n\\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and\nexactly one line (the line \\(S\\)) is sunny.  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\nFrom §§ 3–5 we have proved:\n\n* No configuration exists with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines.\n* Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\